{
 "title": "Sequentially valid inference for probabilistic inflation forecasts - Replication Spec",
 "hypothesis": "Probabilistic inflation forecasts are probabilistically/rank calibrated uniformly distributed PIT/ranks; sequential e-value tests detect time-varying miscalibration missed by static full-sample KS tests.",
 "data_sources": [
  "FRED-MD",
  "ECB Statistical Data Warehouse (EA-MD-QD)",
  "SNB data portal"
 ],
 "sample": "Monthly year-over-year inflation pi_t = P_t/P_{t-12}-1. Harmonized predictor sets ~20 series per region (monetary/credit aggregates, interest rates, consumer/producer prices). Retained only monthly series free of missing obs over sample period. Transformations per McCracken FRED-MD/ECB/SNB to stationarity. Inflation not winsorized; all stationary predictors winsorized at 0.5th and 99.5th percentiles. US: CPIAUCSL. EA: HICP. CH: Swiss CPI.",
 "period_start": "January 1960 (US), January 1983 (Switzerland), January 2001 (Euro Area)",
 "period_end": "April 2025",
 "method": [
  "1. Construct pi_t per (1). Apply per-variable transformations as in Table us_data/ea_data/ch_data: Delta, Delta log, 100*Delta log, Delta^2 log as listed. Winsorize predictors at 0.5% and 99.5%.",
  "2. Forecast design: initial training window = first 40% of each region sample. Expanding window: at each t re-estimate model parameters using all data to t; issue h-step predictive CDF F_hat_{t,h} for h in {1,3,6,12}. Re-tune hyperparameters (lags) when cumulative sample reaches 50%,60%,70%,80%,90% of region sample; otherwise keep hyperparameters fixed. Parameter: training=40%, retune_grid=50-90 step 10%, L=20 for PNC/Rolling mean.",
  "3. Estimate models per Appendix models: PNC with L=20 empirical CDF; Gaussian no-change N(pi_t, sigma_h^2) with sigma_h^2 from mean squared h-step residuals expanding window; Rolling mean N(bar_pi_t, sigma_h^2) with bar_pi_t mean last L=20 and variance scaled by sqrt(1+1/L); ARIMA selection: ADF for d, BIC search p<=5 q<=5, adopted ARIMA(1,1,0) Delta pi_t = alpha0 + phi1 Delta pi_{t-1}+eps; BVAR(1) with p=1 chosen by BIC over 1..12, specifications diffuse/Minnesota/Normal-Wishart, iterated forecasts cumulating predicted changes with cumulative variance sum_{1..h} var; DFM state-space y_t=Lambda f_t+eps_t, f_t=VAR(p) with k by Onatski eigenvalue ratio, q by Bai 2007, p=1, EM ML, Kalman filter, cumulated level forecast N(pi_hat, sigma_h^2); DRF: predictors = current, first lag, first difference per macro variable plus pi_t..pi_{t-11}, forest weights w_x(i)=1/N sum_b 1{i in L_b(x)}/|L_b(x)|, predictive CDF = sum w_t(i)1{pi_{i+h}<=y}, default MMD kernel Gaussian, standard hyperparameters.",
  "4. Calibration assessment: For Gaussian models (Naive last, Rolling mean, ARIMA(1,1,0), BVAR diff/Minnesota/NW, DFM) compute PIT z_t = F_hat_{t,h}(pi_{t+h}); For PNC compute rank r_t =1+sum 1{X_i<Y}; For DRF compute randomized PIT. Use 20 bins for histogram. Null: z_t ~ UNIF(0,1) or r_t ~ UNIF{1..m} conditional on t-h information.",
  "5. Sequential e-values: Alternative beta family for PIT, beta-binomial for ranks. Likelihood ratio E^{alpha,beta}(z)=z^{alpha-1}(1-z)^{beta-1}/B(alpha,beta); E^{alpha,beta}(r)=m*p_{alpha,beta}(r). Adaptive MLE per (2): (alpha_hat_t,beta_hat_t)=argmax sum log p_{alpha,beta}(z_i) using past data. Sequential e-value E_{t+1}=E^{alpha_hat_t,beta_hat_t}(z_{t+1}) with E1=E2=1. For lag h>1 compute h separate MLEs on subsequences {k+hs} per (3) and E_{k+th}=E^{alpha_hat_t^k,beta_hat_t^k}(z_{k+th}) with E1..E2h=1. Ignore observations at {0,1} in estimation. Parameter: warm-up n0=10 e-values set to 1 before MLE (per subsequence for h>1).",
  "6. Aggregation: For h=1 product e_t=prod_{i=1..t} E_i is test supermartingale. For h>1 aggregated e_t = 1/h sum_{k=1..h} prod_{l in I_k(t)} E_l where I_k(t)={k+hs: s=0..floor((t-k)/h)} per Prop 4.3. Modified stopping rule tau_{alpha,h}=inf{t: 1/(h e log h) sum_k sup_{s<=t} prod_{l in I_k(s)} E_l >=1/alpha}. Parameter: threshold 1/alpha=100 for alpha=0.01; penalty factor e log(h) for h>1.",
  "7. Reporting: Plot cumulative e_t on log scale with horizontal lines at 1 and rejection threshold. Estimate alternative PIT density via beta and boundary-corrected kernel over highlighted subsamples. Complement with two-sided one-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov test for UNIF(0,1) on full sample PITs (except PNC). Report KS p, edge frequency %, max e over evaluation period. Evaluation period starts when all models have non-missing forecasts; series one month shorter for models requiring differencing."
 ],
